meta {
  name: Export Lab Tests (Excel)
  type: http
  seq: 20
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/master-data/{{masterDataId}}/lab-tests/export
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

vars:pre-request {
  masterDataId: 3bebbd8d-eef4-4bfa-88f3-3aff4a4a4516
}

tests {
  test("200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("returns a tests array", function () {
    const data = res.getBody() && res.getBody().data;
    expect(data).to.have.property("tests");
    expect(data.tests).to.be.an("array");
  });
}

docs {
  Full, unpaginated snapshot of every **active** lab test in a master data,
  composed with its samples and result parameters (each carrying its own
  reference ranges/values) — the source data the frontend turns into the
  "Lab Tests" Excel workbook (see **Import Lab Tests (Excel)** below).

  Returns `{ "tests": [ { ...LabTest scalars, departmentName, categoryName,
  subCategoryName, samples: [...], resultParams: [ { ...scalars,
  reflexTestNames, referenceRanges: [...], referenceValues: [...] } ] } ] }`.

  No pagination — every active test in the master data is returned in one
  call. Classification names (`departmentName`/`categoryName`/
  `subCategoryName`) are resolved server-side so the exported sheet is
  human-editable without a separate lookup call. Each `versionHistory`
  entry's `modifiedBy`/`approvedBy` is likewise resolved from a raw
  `Person.id` to a display name (`firstName [middleName] lastName`) server-
  side — falls back to the raw id only if the person can't be found.

  ## The Excel format

  The frontend builds **one flat worksheet** named `Lab Tests` from this
  payload: a blank row 1, a 120-column header row 2, then one or more data
  rows per test starting at row 3. 117 of those columns are the exact
  set/order the business team specified — several header labels
  intentionally repeat, e.g. "Parameter Name"/"Method"/"GENDER" appear once
  in the Result Parameter block and again in the Reference Range block and
  again in the Reference Value block. 3 columns were added on top of the
  reference format: **"Mandatory Department"** (right after "Mandatory
  Test") and **"Repeat Interval Value"**/**"Repeat Interval Unit"** (right
  after "Repeat Interval Restriction") — the reference file had no column
  for these, so setting "Mandatory Test"/"Repeat Interval Restriction" to
  Yes via Excel was a dead end (`assertCoreInvariants` requires the paired
  field and rejects with no way to fix it — confirmed by a real user hitting
  exactly this).

  A test spans **multiple physical rows** when it has more than one Sample,
  Result Parameter, Reference Range, or Reference Value:
  - Test-level scalar columns (Test Name → Test Status, plus the Notes block
    and read-only Version Control columns) are written once and **vertically
    merged** across the test's full row-span, so they visually read as one
    cell instead of being blank on continuation rows. A merged cell is
    **center-aligned** (horizontal + vertical center, wrap text) so it reads
    clearly as one grouped value spanning several rows.
  - **Samples** align positionally within the test's row-span (the Nth
    sample's columns land on the Nth row) and are left as separate,
    unmerged, **top-aligned** rows — each row is a genuinely distinct sample
    the user can edit independently, never merged even if two samples
    happen to share the same value.
  - **Result Parameters** (and their nested Reference Ranges/Values) form
    **contiguous blocks**: a parameter's own columns populate its first row
    and are **vertically merged and center-aligned** across just that
    parameter's own range/value sub-block (not the whole test); its
    Reference Ranges/Values stay one-per-row, unmerged, top-aligned, since
    each is genuinely distinct, independently-editable data.
  - A test's total row count = `max(samples needed, rows needed to lay out
    all parameters+ranges+values contiguously)`; whichever track is shorter
    is just blank on the extra rows.
  - **Merging is structural, never value-based**: only the columns the
    row-expansion algorithm inherently writes once per test/parameter are
    merged. Two rows with coincidentally identical Sample/Reference Range/
    Reference Value data are never merged — only genuine category-level
    fields (test identity/pricing/TAT/flags/notes, or a parameter's own
    identity/config) are.

  Round-trip this file unmodified through **Import Lab Tests (Excel)** to
  confirm `{ created: 0, updated: <same count> }` with no errors.
}
